<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Choosing the language of your document>

  <TeXmacs> needs to know the language in which your document is written:
  this language determines the rules for breaking words at the end of the
  lines (<em|hyphenation>), the dictionary used for checking the spelling,
  the shape of the quotation marks and of other punctuation, and the words
  which <TeXmacs> inserts by itself, such as \PTheorem\Q, \PChapter\Q or
  \PTable of contents\Q.

  The language of the whole document is chosen in the menu
  <menu|Document|Language>. For instance, after selecting
  <menu|Document|Language|French>, the theorems of your document are
  automatically called \PTh\<#E9\>or\<#E8\>me\Q, and the words are hyphenated following
  the French rules.

  You may also write a single passage in another language, for instance a
  quotation, by selecting it and choosing the language in
  <menu|Insert|Language>. When the cursor is inside such a passage, the left
  part of the status bar shows its language instead of
  <verbatim|text>.

  Notice that the language of your document is unrelated to the language of
  the menus and of the messages of <TeXmacs>, the language of the <em|user
  interface>. The latter can be chosen in the <menu|General> tab of the
  preferences (<menu|Edit|Preferences>; on <name|macOS>, the preferences
  are in the <menu|TeXmacs> menu), under <menu|User interface language>. If you change it, all
  menus are translated at once, for instance <menu|File> becomes
  <menu|Fichier> in French.

